<Page
	title="Corrections"
	description="How to report an error to SpaceNews, what counts as a correction, and how corrections and updates appear on the article."
	eyebrow="Corrections Policy"
	updated="November 8, 2026"
>
	<p>
		SpaceNews aims to get every fact right, and to fix it openly when we don't. This page explains
		how to report an error, what we correct, and how a correction appears on the article itself.
		Corrections are part of our <a href="/editorial-policy">Editorial Policy</a> — not an
		afterthought.
	</p>

	<h2>How to Report an Error</h2>
	<p>
		Email us at <a href="mailto:SpaceNews@info.com">SpaceNews@info.com</a> or use the
		<a href="/contact">contact page</a>. To help us act quickly, please include:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>The article's URL and the sentence or detail you believe is wrong.</li>
		<li>What the correct information is, and — if you have one — a source that supports it, such as an agency page, official statement, or published paper.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		You do not need to be an expert or the subject of the story to report an error. Every report
		is read, and we aim to review each one within two business days.
	</p>

	<h2>What We Correct</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Factual errors</strong> — wrong dates, times, figures, names, places, or outcomes.</li>
		<li><strong>Misleading framing</strong> — a headline, summary, or caption that claims more than the reporting supports.</li>
		<li><strong>Misattribution</strong> — a quotation, claim, or assessment credited to the wrong source, or analyst assessment presented as official confirmation.</li>
		<li><strong>Stale schedule information</strong> — launch dates or mission plans that have officially changed since publication.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Minor typographical errors and formatting problems are fixed directly and do not require a
		correction note.
	</p>

	<h2>How Corrections Appear</h2>
	<p>
		When a significant error is confirmed, we fix the article and add a dated correction note to
		the article itself, stating what was wrong and what it now says. We do not silently rewrite
		substantive errors, and we do not delete articles to hide mistakes — an article whose central
		claim proves wrong is corrected prominently, and the note stays with it.
	</p>

	<h2>Updates Are Not Corrections</h2>
	<p>
		Spaceflight stories develop: a launch is delayed, a mission returns new data, an agency
		revises a plan. When we add new information to a published story we treat it as an update,
		not a correction, and developing events are usually covered in follow-up articles rather than
		by rewriting the original report. The original article's account of what was known at the
		time it was published is left intact, so the record of the coverage stays honest.
	</p>

	<h2>If We Decline to Correct</h2>
	<p>
		If we check a report and conclude the article is accurate, we will tell you what we checked
		and which source supports the published version. Disagreement about emphasis or interpretation
		is not by itself an error, but we are always willing to add clarifying context where a passage
		genuinely misleads.
	</p>
</Page>
